WEBVTT

NOTE
Synthetic platform-neutral daily standup transcript
Sprint 01 | Day 03 of 10 | 2026-04-08 | Focus: Saved-cart merge

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:57.000
<v Jane Doe>Morning, folks. Day 03 of the sprint. We’re moving into saved carts. The goal is a guest cart that survives sign-in without losing items. John, did we settle duplicate quantities?

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:59.000
<v John Doe>Add them, capped at available stock. I thought that was in the story. It’s in my notes, not the acceptance criteria, so I’ll add it.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:03:01.000
<v Michael Brown>My draft replaces the guest cart with the saved one. I’ll change that, but I need to know what the API returns when one item can’t merge.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:04:03.000
<v John Doe>Keep it in the cart and mark unavailable. Don’t drop it silently.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:57.000
<v David Wilson>I’ll need line-level results then. A single success boolean won’t tell me which item needs attention.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:06:07.000
<v Alex Smith>Agreed. Michael, include merged, capped, and unavailable for each line. Keep the response stable; the page is already being built against it.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:07:09.000
<v Michael Brown>I can do that. The inventory batch lookup is another service, though. I don’t have the spec permission yet.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:08:11.000
<v Emily Davis>I requested it yesterday. It’s still pending with the old service owner, who moved teams.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:09:13.000
<v Robert Taylor>I can see the request. I’ll route it to the new owner. The access page hasn’t been updated since the team change.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:10:15.000
<v Sarah Miller>I’ll test duplicate SKU and cap behavior with the duplicate-cart cases. Is the merge safe to retry if the browser times out?

00:10:13.000 --> 00:11:09.000
<v Michael Brown>Good question. I hadn’t handled that. I’ll return a merge ID so the client can fetch the result after a timeout.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:12:19.000
<v David Wilson>Then I need a status endpoint too. Otherwise I might submit the merge twice.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:13:21.000
<v Alex Smith>Yes, but keep the first version small: create merge, return ID, fetch result. We can add polling only if it’s actually needed.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:14:23.000
<v John Doe>One more thing: don’t block the whole merge if one item is unavailable. The others should still come through.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:15:25.000
<v Michael Brown>That fits the per-line response. I’ll update the contract before lunch.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:16:27.000
<v Jane Doe>We’ve had a few contract changes arrive only in notes. Let’s do a ten-minute walkthrough tomorrow with frontend, backend, QA. I’ll send the link and check everyone can make it.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:17:21.000
<v David Wilson>I can make it unless the accessibility review gets moved again. Last time I joined halfway through.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:18:31.000
<v Jane Doe>That was my scheduling mistake. I’ll check calendars before I move it.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:19:33.000
<v Emily Davis>I can review the inventory query once the permission lands. I’m also finishing the catalog performance rerun, so not both today.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:20:35.000
<v Alex Smith>Take the performance rerun first; it’s in the release gate. I’ll ask someone else to review the merge response.
